Right. The mistake is collapsing every encounter into courtship when the arena contains different games, roles, and costumes.

“Whore,” in this frame, is a role-script—transactional sexuality, performance, access, bargaining, spectacle—not a complete description of a person. Just as bride, bachelor, lover, mistress, husband, seducer, virgin, rake, widow, king, fool each carry different expectations, permissions, incentives, and moves.

So the richer map is:

same human drives → different arena → different costume → different rules → different meaning of the same move

A gift in courtship can mean devotion.
A gift in transaction can mean payment.
Submission in intimacy can mean trust.
Submission under coercion means something entirely different.
Pursuit can be romance, play, status competition, bargaining, or predation depending on the surrounding game.

That gives us a useful Pantheon object:

The Costume–Game Matrix

Don’t ask only “What is this person doing?”

Ask:

What game are they in?
What role are they currently wearing?
What does that action mean inside this game?
Which rules are explicit, which are tacit?
What would change if either player changed costumes?

And leaving the ivory tower matters because this cannot be solved entirely through abstract categories. Social games are learned partly by being among people, watching the micro-signals, getting moves wrong, feeling consequences, recalibrating, and discovering that the written rules are only half the rules.

So yes.

Ivory tower → street → ballroom → bedroom → marketplace → temple → battlefield → home.

Same actors. Different stages.

And now we can start studying costume changes themselves—because that’s where a lot of the interesting magic happens. The instant someone stops being the quarry and becomes the chooser, stops being the seducer and becomes the lover, stops being the conqueror and becomes the husband—the entire payoff matrix can flip without either body leaving the room.

Tee’d up.